KNOWNBUG: std::erase_if over a populated std::vector still leaves the size
assertion failing, but the remaining cause is now narrow and well isolated.

Layers already fixed (each independently regression-tested):
  * convert_function return-type exception-safety crash
    (cpp20_erase_if_return_type).
  * iterator -> const_iterator user-defined conversion via a class-template-id
    converting constructor during member-call overload resolution
    (cpp20_member_overload_converting_ctor) -- std::erase_if's body now converts
    and runs, there is no "found no match for symbol 'erase'".
  * if-statement declaration conditions (cpp_if_declaration_condition) -- this
    fixed libstdc++ vector::_M_erase_at_end's
    `if (size_type __n = _M_finish - __pos)`, so clear(), resize() and the
    two-iterator erase(first, last) now correctly shrink the vector.

Verified working now (header-free / <vector> probes): single-element and range
vector::erase, clear(), resize(), pop_back(), iterator arithmetic, std::remove,
and std::remove_if / std::count_if with the predicate passed *directly*.

Remaining functional gap: std::erase_if passes its predicate as
`std::ref(__pred)` to std::__remove_if.  Any standard algorithm invoked with a
std::reference_wrapper callable fails (e.g. std::count_if(v.begin(), v.end(),
std::ref(pred)) miscounts), so __remove_if removes nothing and erase_if is a
no-op.  Localized further: std::addressof works and a hand-written
"pointer-to-lambda + operator()" wrapper works, and a perfect-forwarding call
`std::forward<F>(f)(args)` works, but std::invoke(callable, args) /
std::__invoke does not actually invoke the callable.  The gap is therefore in
std::__invoke's tag-dispatch / __invoke_result trait machinery for the
callable-object case (the path reference_wrapper::operator() uses), not in
overload resolution, addressof, or forwarding.

Bounded with --unwind to keep this KNOWNBUG fast.  Reclassify CORE once
std::__invoke invokes a callable object so std::ref'd predicates work and
erase_if shrinks the populated vector to size() == 2.
